    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Jean Baptiste Théophile, also known as Théophile Bascle (died 1882); his estate sale, Hotel Drouot, Paris, April 12-14, 1883, lot 65 [according to Hebert sale catalogue].  Madame Hebert, Paris; her estate sale, Hotel Drouot, Paris, March 15, 1926, lot 45 (ill.), to Dru [according to Gazette de l'Hôtel Drouot].  Van Gelder, Paris, by 1930 to at least 1932  [lent to The Hague and Amsterdam exh.; see also Roger-Marx 1932].  John Dorus Van Itallie, New Jersey [information provided by Lock Galleries].  Lock Galleries, New York; sold to the Art Institute, 1968.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435438",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Provenance Statement",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "Johan Barthold Jongkind brought a new spirit to the painting of coastal scenes. Claude Monet claimed that it was Jongkind who “educated his eye,” and his content, palette, and technique played an important part in the development of Impressionism. Though Jongkind painted his final compositions in the studio, the artist strove to retain the freshness of his plein-air (outdoor) sketches.",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300026032",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Summary",
          "classified_as": [
            {
              "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300418049",
              "type": "Type",
              "_label": "Brief Text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "LinguisticObject",
      "content": "<p>Along with <a href=\"https://www.artic.edu/artists/40454\">Eugène-Louis Boudin</a> (see his <em>Approaching Storm</em>), Johan Barthold Jongkind brought a new spirit to the painting of coastal scenes. In content, palette, and technique, the works of these two older artists played an important part in the development of Impressionism. <a href=\"https://www.artic.edu/artists/35809\">Claude Monet</a> knew Boudin well and adopted him as his mentor, but he claimed that it was Jongkind who “educated his eye.” Though they painted their final compositions in the studio, both Boudin and Jongkind strove to retain the freshness of their plein-air (outdoor) sketches.</p>\n",
      "classified_as": [
        {
          "id": "http://vocab.getty.edu/aat/300435416",
          "type": "Type",
          "_label": "Description",
          "classified_as": [
